On your earlier question: in production, yes, this loop would usually move at least partly to the backend. Not because the protocol changes, but because the execution location changes.
Typical production split:
frontend:collects prompt
renders transcript
shows tool traces and UI state

backend:holds provider credentials
sends/receives provider payloads
runs the provider turn loop
may execute backend-safe Unicorn tools

frontend and backend together:share the same contract shape we just defined

So the important part is: the protocol stays the same. Only where it runs changes.
Right now, though, we are still correctly staying client-side.
The next good implementation step is probably one of these:
teach the mock provider a few more domain-shaped intents like:
select all ... taxa
focus on ...
show me details for ...

add richer tool schemas so future real providers know the expected arguments more clearly

begin the first real OpenAI adapter

I’d recommend 1 first for a bit more UX power before we wire a real provider.
